  "query_preview": "..Current radar shows scattered showers east of\nTucson with a few embedded weak thunderstorms. To the south in\nSonora there is a large decaying MCS. CAMs show this MCS sending\nan outflow into southern Arizona later this morning, but will\nlikely not lead to any storm formation, just send some\nreinforcing surface moisture. Aloft high pressure is centered\nnear Oklahoma and Arkansas and a trough is sitting over the west\ncoast. The upper air pattern will more or less remain in this\nstate with the ridge well to the east and a trough just to the\nwest. \n\nAmple moisture will again be in place today with scattered\nthunderstorms forming later this afternoon through the evening\nhours. Could be a little delayed depending on how long the \ncurrent shower activity lasts. Coverage looks to be similar to \nSaturday with the main threat being heavy rainfall and flash \nflooding. There were areas west of Tucson, over the eastern \nportion of Tohono O'Odham Nation, that received 2-3 inches of \nrain Saturday according to radar estimates. The 30/00Z HREF \nshows this same area, from central Pima County to western Graham\nand Cochise Counties, under threat of similar rainfall amounts.\nA Flash Flood Watch is in effect for these areas through early \nMonday morning. Areas that received rainfall yesterday will be \nat greater risk of flash flooding today. There will also be a \nthreat of strong to severe wind gusts, but the greater threat \nwill still be heavy rainfall. Convection could continue through\nSunday night as a couple shortwaves move through Arizona ahead \nof the main trough.\n\nNocturnal convection could delay or decrease storm coverage and\nintensity Monday. But CAMs still show scattered showers and\nthunderstorms over most of southeast Arizona with the main\nthreat heavy rainfall. The HREF again paints areas just west of\nTucson and south with the best chances for rainfall totals \n1\"-2\".\n\nAfter Monday PWATs decrease by about 0.25\" leading to an\nexpected downturn in monsoon activity. Southeast Arizona stays\nin a transitional type of pattern with southwesterly mid-level\nwinds. Tuesday and Wednesday expect isolated coverage of storms\nmainly south and east of Tucson. Later in the week models show a\npossible tropical system moving parallel to the west coast of\nBaja a few hundred miles offshore. This could send tropical \nmoisture our way as early as Thursday leading to an uptick in \nthunderstorm activity. Still a little ways out with lots of \nuncertainty in the track of the tropical system. But ensembles \nare showing a 40-70 percent chance for PWATs to be above 1.5\" \nfrom Tucson westward Friday into next weekend. \n\nHigh temperatures will cool to around normal today, 2-6 degrees\nbelow normal Monday and Tuesday, then back to around normal the\nrest of the work week.",
